.TH CHASHMAP_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chashmap_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at a hash map's first entry
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

cmap_iterator *chashmap_begin_iter(chmap chm, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator positioned at the map's first entry. Each step
gives the key and the value of one entry. The caller reads them with
the unified accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3).
The value pointer points at the map's own storage. A write through it
therefore updates the stored value in place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro selects this function for a chmap.
Call this function directly only when the container type is already
known and the caller wants the error string. ccol_begin(3) is the
ordinary entry point.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm   Hash map to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure, or NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to a newly allocated iterator positioned at the first
entry.
Returns NULL when there is nothing to iterate (chm is NULL or the map
holds no entries), with *err set to NULL.
Returns NULL with *err set to an error string when the iterator itself
could not be allocated.
.fi
.SH "ITERATION ORDER"
.nf
The order follows the map's implementation strategy, which
chmap_create_full(3) describes. For both strategies the order is not
part of the map's contract. Do not depend on it.

Separate chaining keeps a list of every live entry. It links each new
entry at the head of that list. The iterator therefore visits the
newest entry first and works back to the oldest one.

Open addressing has no such list. It walks the slot array from the
first slot to the last one. The order therefore follows where each key
hashed to, and not when the caller inserted it. The hash depends on the
mode of the map, and the keyed mode depends on a secret of the process,
so that order changes when the map switches to the keyed mode and then
differs from one process to the next. See chmap_create_full(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL at entry, before anything else. Test
*err, and not the return value, to tell an empty result apart from a
failure
A NULL chm iterates as an empty map. It does not report an error.
This is a deliberate cross-module contract. cvector_begin_iter(3) and
cbmap_begin_iter(3) share it. The caller can therefore iterate a
lazily created map field that is still NULL because nothing is in it
yet. No caller needs to write its own NULL guard first
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
entry and returns NULL. An iteration that stops early must end with
ccol_iter_destroy(3). The caller can also declare the iterator with
ccol_iter_declare(3), so that scope exit destroys it
The iterator comes from the map's own memory management procedures.
This is why chmap_destroy_with_dtor(3) is the better way to destroy a
map whose values need a destructor for each entry. That function walks
the map with no allocation at all. An iterator that does the same work
can fail under memory pressure
An insert into the map, or a removal from it, invalidates an open
iterator. A change of a value in place through ccol_iter_val_ptr(3)
does not
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(scores, char *, int);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(scores, it);
for (it = chashmap_begin_iter(scores, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%s = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy_with_dtor (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cvector_begin_iter (3)
